# Type design

- Model domain states precisely; prefer tagged unions over boolean flags and loosely related optional fields.
- Prefer specific string literals and unions over broad `string` when the valid set is known.
- Prefer `unknown` over `any` at unsafe boundaries.
- Avoid wrapper object types like `String`, `Number`, and `Boolean`; use primitives.
- Avoid wide index signatures when `Record`, mapped types, or explicit properties are sufficient.
- Keep public types consistent and intentional; avoid duplicate shapes with slightly different meanings.
- Introduce generics only when they express a real relationship between inputs and outputs.
